---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ProcessFlow from '../components/ProcessFlow.astro';
import ToolCard from '../components/ToolCard.astro';
import { tools } from '../data/tools';
import { url } from '../utils/url';

const problems = [
  {
    title: 'Meaning gets lost in translation',
    body: 'A phrase can shift with the language, the tone, the region, or the gesture that goes with it. Word-for-word translation keeps the words and drops the rest: rapport, tone, code-switching, and the gap between what someone says and what they mean.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Every finding looks equally certain',
    body: 'Nothing in a typical research pipeline marks whether a finding is what a person meant or one interpreter’s best guess. It travels with the same authority however many hops of translation or single-source judgment calls produced it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Generic personas result',
    body: 'By the time a finding reaches a design team, meaning has quietly become approximation, and the interface gets built for an averaged, invented person rather than anyone real.',
  },
];

const answers = [
  {
    title: 'Capture faithfully',
    body: 'Meaning Cards and a printable field kit keep what was said, what was observed, and what the researcher infers apart, in the language it was spoken.',
    slug: 'meaning-card',
  },
  {
    title: 'Tag how reliable it is',
    body: 'The Fidelity Protocol records the source language, who interpreted, how it was rendered, and whether a second reader agrees. Missing any of these means no full confidence.',
    slug: 'fidelity-protocol',
  },
  {
    title: 'Test before it ships',
    body: 'The Language Lens Audit checks interface copy for wording, for proxy use, and for trust, with the people who will actually meet it.',
    slug: 'language-lens-audit',
  },
  {
    title: 'Build a record that compounds',
    body: 'Validated moments and tested patterns go into two libraries. Each design pattern cites the evidence behind it, so the next project does not start from zero.',
    slug: 'expression-library',
  },
];

const applies = [
  'Public-service and civic platforms in any linguistically diverse country',
  'Healthcare intake and patient-facing tools used with immigrant or refugee populations',
  'Financial-inclusion products, banking apps, and lending platforms',
  'E-commerce, delivery, and logistics across dialect-rich regions',
  'Humanitarian and NGO field tools in crisis zones with no shared language',
  'Enterprise software used by distributed teams working in a second or third language',
  'Voice assistants, IVR systems, and chatbots deployed far outside the dialect they were trained on',
];
---

<BaseLayout>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">A toolkit for cross-language field research</p>
      <h1>Keep what people mean, not only what the words say.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The Meaning-to-Interface Toolkit helps research teams capture emotional and cultural meaning faithfully across
        languages, tag how reliable each finding is, and carry it through to interface wording that has actually been tested.
      </p>
      <p class="built">
        Built for research on first-generation Indian college students. Reusable for any cross-language qualitative research.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn" href="#process">See the process</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={url('/downloads/')}>Download the templates</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="problem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2 id="problem">Cross-language research loses exactly the thing that matters most.</h2>
        <p>
          Run a conversation through a translator or transcriber once and the nuance is gone by the time it reaches a design team.
          Nobody downstream can tell what was meant from what was guessed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--3">
        {
          problems.map((p) => (
            <article class="card">
              <h3>{p.title}</h3>
              <p>{p.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="answer">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">What the toolkit does</p>
        <h2 id="answer">Make the attrition of meaning visible, then stop it.</h2>
        <p>
          Every finding is tagged with how much it was translated, by whom, and whether it was confirmed by a second reader. Every
          design pattern traces back to the exact moment that justified it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--4">
        {
          answers.map((a) => (
            <article class="card">
              <h3>{a.title}</h3>
              <p>{a.body}</p>
              <p class="more">
                <a href={url(`/tools/${a.slug}/`)}>Learn more</a>
              </p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="process" aria-labelledby="process-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The process</p>
        <h2 id="process-heading">Three modules, an entry step, and a loop.</h2>
        <p>
          Module 1 builds a picture of the setting. Module 2 captures meaning moments one person at a time. Module 3 turns raw
          moments into validated meaning, then into a tested design response. The last step feeds new questions back into
          Module 1, so the process compounds across research cycles.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ProcessFlow />
      <p class="funnel">
        Not every Meaning Card travels all the way to a shipped pattern. Many surface something useful without further action, a
        smaller number justify a design hypothesis, and fewer still become tested, library-worthy patterns. A “not yet” or a
        “no” at a decision point is the toolkit doing its job.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="tools-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The toolkit</p>
        <h2 id="tools-heading">Six tools, one thread of evidence.</h2>
        <p>Each tool has its own page, with the printable and digital templates you can download.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--3">
        {tools.map((t, i) => <ToolCard tool={t} index={i + 1} />)}
      </div>
      <p class="more-row"><a class="btn btn--ghost" href={url('/tools/')}>Tools overview</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="where">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Where to use it</p>
        <h2 id="where">Not specific to one sector, or to India.</h2>
        <p>
          The condition that makes this toolkit necessary is simple: somewhere between the people building a product and the
          people using it, there is a language or meaning gap, and someone is bridging it by guesswork rather than a documented
          method.
        </p>
        <p>
          The common thread is a research or design team on one side of a language boundary and real users on the other, with no
          method in place for checking whether meaning crossed.
        </p>
        <p><a href={url('/about/')}>Read the methodology</a></p>
      </div>
      <ul class="applies">
        {applies.map((a) => <li>{a}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-block: clamp(3rem, 9vw, 6rem) clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    background:
      radial-gradient(60rem 24rem at 85% -10%, var(--accent-soft), transparent 70%),
      var(--bg);
  }
  .hero h1 {
    max-width: 18ch;
  }
  .built {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }
  .more {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
  }
  .funnel {
    max-width: var(--prose);
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .more-row {
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
  }
  .two {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem 3.5rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
    align-items: start;
  }
  .applies {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  .applies li {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-size: 0.97rem;
  }
</style>
